# PART XII — HISTORICAL TELEMETRY AND FORENSIC TIME MACHINE

## 56. Build a reliable telemetry history

Extend the existing history and Time Machine components so they are based on stored observations.

Provide historical views for:

- CPU and memory.
- Disk use.
- DNS timing.
- TCP connection timing.
- Probe success rate.
- Service status.
- Topology changes.
- Node reachability.
- Incident status.
- Action execution.
- Verification outcomes.
- Confidence or hypothesis changes.

Do not show a continuous historical line if only one or two samples exist.

Show the sample count, actual time span, missing intervals, and data freshness.

A gap in data is not proof that the system was healthy.

## 57. Create before-and-after incident analysis

For a remediation, capture:

- Baseline measurements.
- Timestamp of proposed action.
- Timestamp of approval.
- Actual execution time.
- Post-action measurements.
- Follow-up measurements.
- Changes in relevant dependencies.
- Verification status.
- Any unrelated changes or measurement limitations.

Use appropriate observation windows.

Do not claim causation solely because a metric improved after an action.

Where possible, compare relevant metrics against a recent baseline and identify whether the change is statistically or operationally meaningful.

For short-lived tests, report the sample count and limitations.

## 58. Enable forensic incident replay

Provide a replay interface that can navigate through real recorded observations.

A replay timeline should identify:

- Real events.
- Collected measurements.
- Derived findings.
- Operator actions.
- Simulation-only experiments.
- Missing intervals.
- Snapshot boundaries.
- Evidence invalidation.
- Incident closure.

A replay must reproduce a historical record, not invent missing events to create a smooth animation.

If no data exists for a period, show the gap.

Allow the operator to inspect the evidence and causal hypothesis that was current at a particular timestamp.

Do not overwrite old causal explanations when the current diagnosis changes.

