# PART XIII — STATISTICAL INTELLIGENCE AND FORECASTING

## 59. Replace arbitrary predictive claims with measured forecasting

Build forecasting features that use actual samples and document their limitations.

Potential methods include:

- Rolling averages.
- Median and percentile-based latency.
- Exponential smoothing.
- Robust anomaly thresholds.
- Trend estimates.
- Change-point indicators.
- Seasonal patterns when enough history exists.
- Baseline deviation.
- Error-rate windows.
- Resource saturation forecasts when sufficient data exists.

Choose methods appropriate to the available sample count.

Do not forecast a meaningful outage probability from one measurement.

Do not invent historical data to make a forecasting panel appear active.

When there are insufficient observations, show a baseline collection requirement rather than a fabricated prediction.

## 60. Add anomaly detection

Implement a reusable anomaly framework.

An anomaly result should contain:

- Metric.
- Target.
- Current value.
- Expected baseline.
- Relevant time window.
- Detection method.
- Deviation size.
- Number of samples.
- Confidence limitations.
- Whether the event was confirmed by independent observations.

Start with simple explainable statistical rules.

Use more complex models only when they provide measurable value.

Avoid treating every threshold breach as a root cause.

An anomaly is evidence requiring interpretation, not a final diagnosis.

## 61. Distinguish performance degradation from service failure

Define different incident classes for:

- Hard availability failure.
- High latency.
- Intermittent failures.
- Partial degradation.
- Capacity pressure.
- Configuration divergence.
- Dependency failure.
- Data-quality failure.
- Monitoring failure.
- Unconfirmed warning.

Avoid mapping every negative observation to “critical.”

Use severity based on the impact, affected scope, data quality, and relevant operational policy.

A local sandbox resolver warning should not be displayed as a corporate DNS outage.

