# PART XXI — RELIABILITY ENGINEERING

## 94. Add a circuit-breaker strategy

Prevent repeated failing external services or model providers from consuming excessive resources.

Where appropriate, use states such as:

- Closed.
- Open.
- Half-open.

Document the conditions that trigger transitions.

Do not apply a circuit breaker to ordinary deterministic functions without a clear need.

Expose the status and recovery behavior.

## 95. Add graceful degradation

EJII should remain useful if:

- The model provider is unavailable.
- A remote agent disconnects.
- DNS diagnostics fail.
- Audit storage becomes unavailable.
- The database becomes read-only.
- An external connector times out.
- Historical memory is temporarily unavailable.
- A browser loses its event stream.

Define the expected behavior for each case.

A model outage should not disable deterministic diagnostics.

An audit failure should block governed mutation if required by policy.

A missing remote agent should reduce visibility, not fabricate a healthy remote host.

A transient UI connection loss should not cause the backend to repeat a remediation.

## 96. Add health monitoring for the monitoring system

An infrastructure intelligence product can itself become unhealthy.

Track:

- Last successful telemetry collection.
- Failed collector count.
- Job queue depth.
- Oldest queued job.
- Failed model requests.
- Audit write failures.
- Database errors.
- Migration status.
- Background worker health.
- Memory growth where measurable.
- Resource utilization.
- Last successful integrity check.

Distinguish application health from monitored infrastructure health.

The product should make monitoring blind spots visible.

## 97. Add bounded resource usage

Define limits for:

- Concurrent probes.
- Concurrent investigations.
- Model requests.
- Diagnostic durations.
- Topology traversal.
- Memory retrieval.
- Query result sizes.
- Event retention.
- Snapshot volume.
- History display.
- Simulation complexity.

Prefer explicit budgets over arbitrary sleeps.

Stop operations when the budget is exceeded.

Explain whether a result is complete, partial or truncated.

Do not silently return an incomplete graph and label it complete.

