# PART XXVI — IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS AND ARCHITECTURAL DISCIPLINE

## 121. Use explicit TypeScript or schema contracts where they fit

If the project already has TypeScript contracts, extend them.

Represent investigations, evidence, hypotheses, probes, plans, approvals, outcomes, and memories as typed interfaces or runtime-validated schemas.

Do not duplicate the same schema in many unrelated modules.

Where data crosses a trust boundary, use runtime validation even if the code is statically typed.

Keep database representations and API representations consistent through explicit mapping.

Avoid widespread `any` types where a useful contract can be defined.

Do not introduce a complex type framework just to avoid a handful of straightforward interfaces.

## 122. Use dependency injection for testable services

Where appropriate, allow critical components to receive dependencies such as:

- Database.
- Clock.
- Diagnostic adapters.
- Model gateway.
- Audit service.
- Policy engine.
- Storage interface.

This makes it possible to test error paths without manipulating global modules in fragile ways.

Use a consistent pattern. Do not create a large dependency-injection container if the project does not need one.

Do not let tests monkey-patch shared global dependencies in a way that affects concurrent tests.

## 123. Make operations deterministic where possible

Use deterministic IDs only where appropriate for tests and reproducibility.

Capture timestamps and identifiers from trusted backend code.

Allow the reasoning ranking and graph algorithms to be reproduced using the stored inputs.

Where model generation is stochastic, record the model and task metadata needed to interpret the result.

Do not promise exact replay of an external LLM response unless the system has a way to preserve that result.

A replay should at minimum reconstruct the actual stored investigation state and action history.

## 124. Keep algorithms explainable

Every important algorithm should have:

- A clear purpose.
- Defined inputs.
- Defined outputs.
- Explicit assumptions.
- Bounded complexity.
- Failure behavior.
- Tests.
- Useful diagnostic metadata.

Avoid adding algorithms because their names sound advanced.

A well-tested graph traversal and a simple statistical detector are more valuable than an unverified neural network or a pseudo-Bayesian score with arbitrary constants.

Use complex ML only when real data, validation, and operational benefit justify it.

## 125. Avoid monolithic intelligence modules

The reasoning brain must not become one enormous file with hundreds of unrelated responsibilities.

Organize the system around actual domain boundaries.

Keep prompts in dedicated, versioned templates when useful. Keep schemas separate from prompts. Keep model invocation separate from business logic. Keep tool definitions separate from authorization policy. Keep operational workflows separate from UI rendering.

Do not create tiny modules that add indirection without value.

Every module should have a clear responsibility and a practical test strategy.

## 126. Avoid excessive dependencies

Before adding a library, evaluate:

- Whether an equivalent capability already exists.
- Maintenance activity and compatibility.
- Security implications.
- Installation complexity.
- Runtime resource cost.
- Whether the dependency requires a new server or service.
- Whether it can be tested in the current environment.
- Whether the functionality can be implemented more safely using the current stack.

Do not add a large orchestration framework simply to call a language model once.

Do not add a separate database just for vector search before evaluating the existing storage requirements.

Do not add a heavyweight message broker if a simpler persistence-backed workflow queue is sufficient for the target deployment.

