# PART XXVIII — DEFINITION OF DONE

## 129. Product-level completion

The new intelligence system is not complete until the implemented vertical slice allows an operator to perform an actual workflow from symptom to verified outcome.

At a minimum, that workflow must:

- Accept a meaningful operational question.
- Inspect real available data.
- Preserve evidence and provenance.
- Create structured hypotheses.
- Select a justifiable next probe.
- Execute a permitted diagnostic.
- Update the investigation from the result.
- Retrieve relevant historical information when available.
- Produce a bounded plan.
- Enforce authorization and action policy.
- Execute a real supported action or state clearly why it cannot.
- Verify action-specific postconditions.
- Handle failure and rollback accurately.
- Persist the investigation.
- Render the real state in the UI.
- Produce an evidence-linked summary.

The product should offer useful deterministic behavior without a configured LLM. Additional configured model intelligence should improve explanation, hypothesis generation or planning without overriding deterministic safety controls.

## 130. Security completion

Verify that:

- The port-3000 exception remains removed.
- Non-loopback evaluation mode is rejected.
- Quick-NOC cannot issue administrative tokens.
- The actual request origin and server binding are handled correctly.
- Every sensitive route is authenticated.
- Every state-changing route enforces minimum role and target authorization.
- Client-supplied roles cannot increase privilege.
- Diagnostic targets are validated safely.
- DNS resolution cannot bypass target validation.
- Unregistered capabilities cannot be executed.
- Missing audit infrastructure blocks mutation.
- Failed audit persistence cannot lead to a success state.
- Failed postconditions cannot lead to a verified state.
- Failed rollback verification produces an escalation state.
- Model output cannot bypass action governance.
- Model provider secrets are protected.
- Prompts, logs and evidence are treated as untrusted input.
- Test data cannot alter real operational state.

## 131. Data-integrity completion

Verify that:

- Database migrations are repeatable.
- Test data is isolated.
- Operational history is not deleted by broad text matching.
- Every action has a traceable snapshot when required.
- Snapshot read-after-write verification is meaningful.
- Audit integrity checks use actual records.
- Evidence lineage refers to existing records.
- Historical findings retain their original provenance.
- Simulation data cannot masquerade as live observations.
- Missing measurements remain unknown.
- Modelled values are labelled as modelled.
- Stale observations are not presented as current state.

## 132. Frontend completion

Verify that:

- Every new panel uses real API data.
- All actions have functioning handlers.
- Loading and error states work.
- Empty states work.
- Unknown values are displayed honestly.
- Filters and searches work correctly.
- Historical timelines reflect stored events.
- Investigation details link to their evidence.
- Remediation approvals show the exact plan.
- Post-action status is based on backend results.
- The interface clearly separates live, derived, simulated and unknown information.
- The design remains mature and professional rather than cluttered.

## 133. Verification completion

Run the relevant tests against the final code, not an earlier draft.

At minimum, run:

- The existing build.
- Existing autonomous-operator tests.
- Existing v6 API tests.
- Authentication and RBAC tests.
- Secure-deployment tests.
- New investigation workflow tests.
- New evidence and lineage tests.
- New model gateway tests.
- New probe selection tests.
- New memory retrieval tests.
- New audit failure tests.
- New rollback verification tests.
- New SSRF tests.
- New migration and database isolation tests.
- Frontend interaction tests where browser tooling is available.

Report exact commands, results, and any skipped tests.

Do not state that a full end-to-end browser flow was verified if only unit tests ran.

Do not state that the application is secure merely because the build succeeded.

Do not hide failures behind an overall green summary.

